const description = "Dryer squeaking? Usually the drum rollers, idler pulley, or a worn belt. What each sounds like and what's safe to check. Same-day Los Angeles, $89.";

const faqs = [
  {
    q: "Why is my dryer squeaking?",
    a: "A squeak or squeal almost always comes from a worn moving part the drum rides on or is driven by: the drum support rollers, the idler pulley (which tensions the belt), the drum glides or slides at the front, the drive belt itself, or a rear drum bearing. As these wear, they lose their smooth surface and start to squeal under the drum's weight. Which one it is often depends on the sound — a rhythmic squeak that speeds up with the drum points at rollers or bearings, while a constant high squeal often points at the idler pulley or belt. Some of these are homeowner-accessible; a technician can pinpoint and replace the worn part in one visit. $89 residential diagnostic, waived with repair."
  },
  {
    q: "What's the difference between a squeal and a grinding dryer?",
    a: "It's the difference between 'soon' and 'now.' A squeak or squeal is a worn part still doing its job — the rollers, pulley, glides, or belt are wearing but the drum still turns. Grinding, scraping, or thumping means a part has worn far enough to fail or has come apart: a seized roller flat-spotting, a bearing that's collapsing, or the drum dragging on metal. Once it grinds, stop running the dryer — a seized roller or bearing creates friction and heat, and a dryer already runs hot with lint around, so it's not worth the risk. A squeal you can schedule; a grind you should shut off and get looked at."
  },
  {
    q: "Can I fix a squeaking dryer myself?",
    a: "Sometimes. On many dryers the belt, idler pulley, and drum rollers are reachable once the top or front panel comes off, and those are the usual squeak parts. If you're comfortable unplugging the dryer, opening the cabinet, and you can identify a glazed roller or a cracked belt, it's a doable repair with the right parts. What makes it a tech job: gas dryers (don't disturb the gas line), a rear drum bearing that requires full disassembly, or not being sure which part is worn — replacing the wrong one leaves the squeak. Always unplug first, and on a gas dryer shut off the gas before opening anything."
  },
  {
    q: "Which part squeaks most often on a dryer?",
    a: "The drum support rollers and the idler pulley are the two most common. Rollers are the wheels the drum rests and spins on; they wear flat or dry out and squeal under load, and they're often replaced as a set. The idler pulley keeps tension on the drive belt and spins constantly, so its small bearing wears and squeals — it's an inexpensive part but it's doing a lot of work. Drum glides (front) and a worn belt are next. A tech spins the drum by hand with the belt off to isolate which one is singing, because the fix is different for each."
  },
  {
    q: "Is a squeaking dryer dangerous?",
    a: "A squeak by itself isn't dangerous, but don't ignore it — the same worn parts that squeak can seize, and a seized roller or bearing creates friction and heat inside a machine that's already hot and full of lint. Combined with a lint-clogged vent (the leading cause of dryer fires), that's a real risk. So a squeak is your early warning: get the worn part replaced before it seizes, and while you're at it, make sure the lint filter and vent are clean. If the squeak has already turned to grinding or you smell anything hot, stop running it and call."
  }
];

