<!-- 01 ANSWER-FIRST -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">01 · The short answer</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">A squeaking dryer is a worn part the drum rides on or is driven by.</h2>

    <p><strong>Dryers squeak because a moving part has worn: the drum support rollers, the idler pulley that tensions the belt, the drum glides at the front, the drive belt itself, or a rear drum bearing. All of them ride under the weight of a spinning drum, and as they wear they lose their smooth surface and squeal. The sound is a clue — a rhythmic squeak that tracks the drum's speed points at rollers or a bearing, while a steady high squeal points at the idler pulley or belt. A squeak isn't an emergency, but it's an early warning: the same parts that squeak can seize, and a seized part makes heat inside a machine already full of lint. Get the worn part replaced before it grinds. Some are DIY-accessible; a tech pinpoints and swaps the right one in one visit. $89 residential diagnostic, waived with repair.</strong></p>

    <p class="callout"><strong>Squeal you can schedule — a grind means stop.</strong> If the squeak has already turned to grinding, scraping, or thumping, shut the dryer off and don't run it. A seized roller or bearing creates friction and heat next to lint. More on that below.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 02 SOUND MAP -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">02 · Read the sound</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">What the squeak is telling you.</h2>

    <p>You can narrow it down before anyone opens the cabinet, because each part has a signature:</p>

    <ul class="diag-list">
      <li><strong>Rhythmic squeak that speeds up with the drum</strong> → drum support rollers or a rear bearing (they turn with the drum).</li>
      <li><strong>Constant high-pitched squeal, doesn't track the drum</strong> → idler pulley or drive belt (they spin fast and steady).</li>
      <li><strong>Squeak worst at start-up, eases as it warms</strong> → often drum glides or a belt that's stiff cold.</li>
      <li><strong>Squeak plus a burning or hot smell</strong> → stop now; a part may be seizing. This is not a schedule-it situation.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>None of this replaces a proper look, but it tells you and the tech where to start — and whether it's a squeal you can book or a grind you should shut off.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 03 CAUSES -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">03 · The parts that squeak</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">The causes, in the order our techs check them.</h2>

    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Drum support rollers (most common)</h3>
      <p>The wheels the drum rests and spins on. They wear flat or dry out and squeal under the drum's weight — usually replaced as a set so the fix lasts. One of the two most common squeak sources.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Idler pulley</h3>
      <p>Keeps tension on the drive belt and spins constantly, so its small bearing wears and squeals. Inexpensive part doing a lot of work — the other most common source.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Drum glides / slides (front)</h3>
      <p>Plastic pads the front of the drum rides on. When they wear thin, the drum rubs metal-on-metal and squeaks, often worst at start-up.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Drive belt</h3>
      <p>A cracked or worn belt can squeal as it slips over the drum and pulley. Sometimes the belt and idler are replaced together.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Rear drum bearing</h3>
      <p>The bearing the back of the drum spins on. When it wears it squeals, then grinds — reaching it means full disassembly, so it's a tech job.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 04 SAFE FIX -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">04 · What you can safely check</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Before it turns to a grind.</h2>

    <ol class="diag-list">
      <li><strong>Unplug the dryer first</strong> — and on a gas dryer, shut off the gas before opening anything.</li>
      <li><strong>Clean the lint filter and check the vent.</strong> A clogged vent doesn't cause the squeak, but it's the leading dryer-fire risk and worth clearing while a worn part is in play.</li>
      <li><strong>Listen at start-up vs. warm</strong> and note whether the squeak tracks the drum — it tells the tech which part to bring.</li>
      <li><strong>If you're comfortable in the cabinet,</strong> the belt, idler pulley, and rollers are reachable on many dryers once the top or front panel is off — a glazed roller or cracked belt is visible.</li>
    </ol>

    <p class="callout"><strong>Where to stop:</strong> the lint/vent and a visual check are safe. Leave a gas dryer's gas line alone, and leave a rear drum bearing (full teardown) to a tech. If it's already grinding or smells hot, don't run it.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 05 DANGER -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">05 · When a squeak becomes a hazard</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Don't run a dryer that's grinding.</h2>

    <p>A squeak is a worn part still working; a grind is a part failing. Once you hear grinding, scraping, or thumping — or smell anything hot — stop running the dryer. A seized roller or collapsing bearing creates friction and heat, and a clogged lint vent is already the leading cause of dryer fires. The two together are exactly the situation to avoid.</p>

    <p>So treat the squeak as the free warning it is: replace the worn part before it seizes, and keep the lint filter and vent clear. If you're not sure whether you're hearing a squeal or a grind, that's a fine reason to have us listen.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 06 TECH + LINKS -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">06 · When to call us</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Squealing, or already grinding? We'll pinpoint the part.</h2>

    <p>Our techs spin the drum by hand with the belt off to isolate exactly which part is singing — rollers, idler, glides, belt, or bearing — and carry the common ones for a one-visit fix. If it's already grinding, we'll get to you before it does more damage.</p>

    <p>More help: the <a href="/services/dryer-repair/not-tumbling/">dryer not tumbling</a> guide (if the drum won't turn at all, it's often the belt), and if the dryer's slow or not heating, <a href="/services/dryer-repair/takes-too-long/">takes too long to dry</a> and <a href="/services/dryer-repair/not-heating/">not heating</a>. Same-day across <a href="/los-angeles/dryer-repair/">Los Angeles</a>, <a href="/beverly-hills/dryer-repair/">Beverly Hills</a>, and <a href="/pasadena/dryer-repair/">Pasadena</a> in most cases. <a href="/book/">Book online</a> or call {phone}.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 07 FAQ -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">07 · FAQ</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Dryer squeaking: common questions.</h2>

    <div class="faq-list">
      {faqs.map(f => (
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>{f.q}</summary>
          <p>{f.a}</p>
        </details>
      ))}
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 08 BOTTOM CTA -->
<section class="bottom-cta">
  <div class="container">
    <h2>Dryer squeaking or grinding? We'll find the worn part.</h2>
    <p>$89 diagnostic waived with repair. Same-day across LA, OC, Ventura. Phones answered 24/7.</p>
    <div class="cta-row">
      <a href={phoneHref} class="btn-primary">☎ Call {phone}</a>
      <a href="/book/" class="btn-secondary">Book Online</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

</Layout>

